  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-8) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+4x-8) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x-2)+4(x-2)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}$ are $x=-4,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$  where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-4$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033945/assets/a357caad0365f296f87367ee4ac1000f1ea772d0d27650c8340d475ca742828b.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
